+## Open-issue repair acceptance
+
+The existing `test:terminal-agent` discovers `localized-picker-contract.test.ts` and the binary downloader regressions. `test:browser` also exercises real local socket teardown, stale-run ownership and ordinary-terminal environment filtering. `test:reliability` covers cold habit indexing and refresh/write ordering. `test:card-panels` includes real Preact saved/loading/error states from `verify-open-issue-panels.ts`. `test:safety-regressions` covers delayed environment shutdown without allocating a new session. These are included in the existing `test:all` gate; no separate test entry is added.
+
+`open-issues.mjs` and `terminal-repair.mjs` under `scripts/obsidian-acceptance/` require `NAND_ALLOW_BROWSER_E2E=1`, a matching `NAND_WINDOWS_E2E_NONCE`, an absolute `NAND_ACCEPTANCE_DIR`, and the isolated Vault marker. `open-issues.mjs` additionally verifies the deployed bundle against `NAND_EXPECT_MAIN_SHA`; it builds a temporary native acceptance plugin from the actual picker sources and removes it after execution. `terminal-repair.mjs` requires an absolute `NAND_ACCEPTANCE_BINARY` pointing to the verified release service. It uses an owned HTTP fault fixture for 404/retry and real PTYs for environment boundaries, never provider credentials. `open-issues.mjs --startup-only` checks the existing habit after a full app restart. These scripts must run serially with other native acceptance. The historical nonce variable name does not establish Windows coverage; record the actual platform.
+
+`scripts/verify-terminal-release-download.mjs` is an explicit online Linux release smoke check, not part of offline `test:all`. Run with the same Node TypeScript hook as terminal tests. It uses the production downloader on a fresh temporary plugin directory, checks the installed version and runs the authenticated PTY/history integration against the downloaded release binary. It does not publish or change a Release.
